course: everyday
lesson: 289
title: Teeth, bath time, eyes, dressing, sleep and safe food
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Many small habits keep the body safe and healthy: cleaning between the teeth with floss, short showers, safety glasses, getting dressed in the right order, a calm bedtime and clean, safe food.
explain: Some dangers are hidden. A small child can drown in just a few centimetres of bath water in seconds, and a whole round grape can block a small child's airway like a cork in a bottle.
explain: Example: a boy in Florida is asked to watch his little sister in the bath while his mum answers the door. He does not leave her alone for a moment: he calls his mum to come back instead.
explain: Sleep is also a habit. The bright light and excitement of a tablet or phone tell the brain to stay awake, so screens go away about an hour before bed, and a dark, quiet, cool room helps deep sleep.
fact: Use dental floss to clean between your teeth; never use a fork, pin or other sharp object, which can cut the gums and chip teeth.
fact: Never leave a small child alone in a bath; call an adult instead.
fact: Small children can drown in a few centimetres of water in a few seconds.
fact: Keep showers short, around five minutes, to save water and energy.
fact: Wear safety glasses when told to during experiments, because chemicals can hurt eyes for life.
fact: Get dressed in order: underwear first, then clothes, then socks and shoes; it is quicker and clothes fit right.
fact: Put screens away about an hour before sleep and keep them out of the bedroom, because their light and excitement make it harder to fall asleep.
fact: A dark, quiet and cool bedroom helps deep sleep.
fact: Wash fruit and vegetables before eating, because they may have dirt or chemicals on them.
fact: Share food with a friend only if neither of you has allergies and adults agree, because some foods are dangerous for people with allergies.
fact: Between meals, a healthy snack like fruit, yoghurt or nuts (if you are not allergic) keeps your energy steady.
fact: Cut grapes lengthways for small children, because small round food can block a small child's airway.
exercise: A piece of meat is stuck between your teeth. What should you use? | Dental floss | The tip of a fork | A sewing pin | A sharp pencil
exercise: Why should you never pick your teeth with a pin? | It can cut your gums and chip your teeth | It makes your teeth grow in crooked over time | It turns your teeth yellow | Pins are only bad for baby teeth
exercise: Your mum asks you to watch your three-year-old brother in the bath, then the phone rings in your room. What do you do? | Stay with him and call an adult; never leave him alone | Go and answer the phone quickly | Leave him, because the water is shallow and he can sit up | Turn the tap on so he has something to do
exercise: How much water can a small child drown in? | Just a few centimetres | Only water deeper than their head | Only water in a swimming pool | Only water over a metre deep
exercise: About how long should a shower be to save water? | Around five minutes | Around thirty minutes | Until the hot water runs out | As long as you like
exercise: Your science teacher says to put on safety glasses before mixing liquids. Why? | Chemicals splashing in your eyes can hurt them for life | The glasses help you read the labels | Safety glasses make the experiment work better and more safely | It is only so the class looks the same
exercise: In which order should you get dressed? | Underwear, then clothes, then socks and shoes | Shoes first, then socks, then clothes | Clothes first, then underwear on top | Socks, then underwear, then shoes, then clothes
exercise: Why is it wise to put your tablet away about an hour before bed? | Its light and excitement make it harder to fall asleep | It helps the battery last longer | Tablets are too heavy to hold up in bed when you are sleepy | It makes your dreams shorter
exercise: Which bedroom is best for deep sleep? | Dark, quiet and cool | Bright, noisy and warm | Hot, with the television on | Light, with music playing loudly
exercise: You buy apples at the shop. What should you do before eating one? | Wash it under clean water | Eat it straight from the bag | Wipe it on your shirt | Polish it with a tissue only
exercise: Why should you wash fruit and vegetables? | They may have dirt or chemicals on them | Washing adds extra vitamins and goodness to them | It makes them taste sweeter | Shops ask you to wash them first
exercise: A friend at school offers you half of her peanut butter sandwich. When is it OK to share? | Only if neither of you has allergies and adults agree | Always, because sharing is kind and good friends share | Only if it looks fresh | Only if you swap something back
exercise: You are hungry two hours before dinner. What is a good snack? | A piece of fruit or a yoghurt | A big bag of sweets | A can of fizzy drink | Nothing at all, ever, until dinner time
exercise: Your baby cousin wants some grapes. How should they be given? | Cut lengthways into smaller pieces | Whole, so they are easy to pick up | Frozen and whole | Two or three at a time
exercise: Why are whole grapes and hard sweets dangerous for small children? | Small round food can block a small child's airway | They are too sour for small children | They give small children a rash | They make small children's teeth fall out much too early
test: A girl in Arizona has popcorn stuck in her teeth and reaches for a safety pin. What should she do instead? | Use dental floss | Use a toothpick made of metal | Use a kitchen knife | Ignore it for a week
test: The bath is only ankle-deep and the toddler is playing happily. Is it safe to leave her alone for "just one minute"? | No, a small child can drown in a few centimetres of water in seconds | Yes, ankle-deep water is always safe for a happy toddler | Yes, if she has bath toys with her | Yes, if the door is left open
test: A family wants to use less water and energy. Which change helps? | Taking shorter showers of about five minutes | Leaving the shower running while choosing clothes | Having a long bath every morning | Washing hands for a whole hour
test: In a school experiment, a boy says safety glasses look silly and takes them off. What is the danger? | A splash could damage his eyes for life | He might not see the colours of the liquids properly | The experiment might not work | There is no danger at all
test: A boy keeps waking up tired because he watches videos in bed until late. What would help most? | Putting screens away an hour before bed, outside the bedroom | Watching funnier videos | Turning the screen brightness right up so his eyes stay open | Watching videos with the light on
test: A girl in Brisbane cannot sleep because her room is hot, bright and noisy. What would help? | Making the room darker, quieter and cooler | Opening the curtains wide to let in the streetlights | Turning on a loud fan heater | Playing loud music to cover the noise
test: Why is it better to put on underwear before your trousers? | Clothes go on in order so they fit right and it is quicker | Underwear is warmer on the outside | Trousers must touch your skin | It does not matter at all which piece of clothing goes first
test: A boy brings a carrot straight from the shop bag to his mouth. What should he have done? | Washed it first, in case of dirt or chemicals | Peeled it with his teeth | Put it in the fridge for an hour | Nothing, because food from a shop is always clean already
test: Your friend has a nut allergy and you have a cookie with nuts. What should you do? | Not share it, and keep it away from your friend | Share a small piece only | Pick the nuts out and share the rest | Let your friend decide alone, without asking any adults
test: Your little brother is two. Your aunt hands him a bowl of whole grapes. What should you do? | Tell an adult; the grapes should be cut lengthways | Let him eat them, because grapes are healthy for little ones | Give him bigger grapes instead | Give him hard sweets instead
test: got food stuck in my teeth, use a fork to get it? | No, use floss, sharp things cut gums | Yes, if you go slowly | Yes, a sewing pin is even better and safer | Only the back of the fork
test: bubs in the bath, i'm just grabbing my phone 1 sec | Don't leave him, call an adult instead | Fine, babies float | Fine if the water's shallow and he can sit up | Fine for under a minute
test: cant sleep, been on my phone in bed for ages | Put screens away an hour before bed | Turn the brightness right up | Watch till you feel tired out and your eyes close | Play a game to tire your eyes
test: cutting grapes for my lil sister, why lengthways | Small round food can block a small child's airway | They taste sweeter and juicier cut that way | It makes them last longer in the lunchbox and the fridge | So they fit on the plate
explain: A mum in England left her toddler in the bath for a moment to answer the door; when she came back he was face-down in the water, and only her fast first aid saved him.
explain: A boy in Arizona used a pin to pick food from his teeth; he cut his gum, it got infected and his face swelled up.
fact: Drowning is quick and silent: a small child in the bath may make no splash or sound at all.
fact: Food left out in the heat can grow germs that cause bad stomach illness.
test: mum's on the phone, my 2yo sister is in the bath alone | Stay with her or call mum back right now | It's fine for a minute | It's fine for a minute while you finish the call | It's fine if she has toys
test: lunch was in the hot car all day, still ok to eat? | No, germs grow in warm food and can make you ill | Yes, if it smells fine | Yes, if you just reheat it a little bit | Yes, sandwiches never go off
test: playing on my tablet in bed till 1am lol | Screens before bed make you tired and grumpy next day | It helps you fall asleep faster and dream more | It doesn't matter at your age | It's fine at weekends only
test: did u hear?? a toddler was left in the bath "for like 10 seconds" while her mum got a towel, and she went under. lesson? | Never leave a small child alone in the bath; call an adult | Ten seconds alone is always safe | Only deep baths are dangerous | Toddlers always splash loudly if they slip, so you'd hear
test: my cousin says someone got food poisoning from potato salad left out at a barbecue and "was in hospital for a month". what's prob exaggerated | The month; still, food left in the heat can grow germs | That food can grow germs | That barbecues are outside | That potato salad left out goes warm
test: kids are laughing that Leo's "allergic to nuts lol, such a baby". pass it on? | No, allergies are serious; never tease or spread it | Yes, he's only making it up for attention | Yes, and hide a nut in his lunch | Yes, it's a harmless joke
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the danger posed to small children by bath water? | They may drown in a few centimetres of water within seconds, without a sound | They are safe provided that the water reaches no higher than the ankle | They always splash and cry out when slipping | They are at risk only in swimming pools
exam: Identify the principal reason for which screens are to be put away approximately one hour prior to sleep. | Their light and excitement make it harder to fall asleep | Screens consume a great deal of electricity during the night | Screens must cool down before charging | Parents require them for their own use
exam: It is given that grapes are to be served to a small child. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | They shall be cut lengthways, since small round food may block the airway | They shall be served whole, since grapes are soft and easily swallowed | They shall be frozen hard before serving | They shall be served only to children who chew loudly
exam: State the correct means of cleaning between the teeth. | Dental floss, and never a fork, pin or other sharp object | A metal pin, held carefully | The tip of a fork, used gently and slowly | A toothpick of sharpened metal
